A||||13||Matthew_Fiero||clutch fluid||||1||
Z||000000||Matthew_Fiero||09-09-2001||10:54 PM||||what kind of fluid should i bleed and use permenantly into the master cylinder?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||09-09-2001||11:02 PM||||DOT 3 brake fluid. <BR>If you get anything other than that, DO NOT MIX FLUIDS!<BR>Using a different fluid requires that the system needs flushed and cleaned for best results!||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||09-09-2001||11:19 PM||||DOT 3 ONLY! Its what the factory recomended, and it works. <P>If you run a search through the archives you will find much discussion about this(particularly ATF fluid as an alternative). DOT 3 is the only thing going in my Fieros anymore.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Matthew_Fiero||09-09-2001||11:25 PM||||**** , i put in 5w30 motor oil in there<BR> thats what i was suggested to do by many people<BR>how do i flush the system?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Matthew_Fiero||09-09-2001||11:31 PM||||i thought my clutch felt a little iwerd, and how i can't shift properly||||reg||1
Z||000005||Indiana_resto_guy||09-10-2001||12:24 AM||||Are you talking about the clutch system (master in the front) or the transmission itself?<BR>If you've put 5W30 in the master cylinder for the clutch . . . . . .OOPS!<BR>The clutch hydraulics are now contaminated and need replaced. (period)||||reg||1
Z||000006||Carrolles||09-10-2001||08:43 AM||||Indy is right.  5W30 goes in the transmission gearcase not in the master cylinder.  Master cylinder gets DOT3 brake fluid.||||reg||1
Z||000007||Matthew_Fiero||09-10-2001||03:53 PM||||do i need a new master and slave cylinder now????||||reg||1
Z||000008||LarryB||09-10-2001||04:47 PM||||At the very least you need to flush out the entire system with the correct brake fluid.<P>Luckily DOT3 fluid is cheap. <P>  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Matthew_Fiero:<BR><B>do i need a new master and slave cylinder now????</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><p>[This message has been edited by LarryB (edited 09-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||09-10-2001||07:04 PM||||The MC and slave must be rebuilt or replaced. The oil cannot be flushed/washed from the rubber parts. You may also have to replace the rubber line in the rear.<P>Oil swells the seals and rubber lining of the soft hoses. This damage cannot be undone except rebuild/replace the damaged part.<P>the line from front to back must be flushed with alcohol while the MC and slave are off. Oil that is left in the line will come back to eat the seals again later. Air used durring flushing must be as dry as posible and MUST NOT have oil in it. <P>You must always use DOT 3 or compatible fluid in brakes and clutch. Valvoline Synthetic brake fluid is compatible with DOT 3 and 4. (Notes about brake fluid are in my cave.)<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||09-10-2001||10:52 PM||||Ogre speaks the truth.<P>When I replaced the clutch in my Formula I decided to replace the master and slave cylinders for good measure. The buzz word back then was 'ATF'(current buzz word is 3.4L). What a mistake that was! I think it was within a week the rubber seals had swelled so much, the p/p springs were almost unable to overcome the rubber cylinder seal pressures. I ended up buying new clutch set, flywheel, master and slave cylinders($$$). I used comressed air to get all the ATF out of the lines, and did the job over.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Matthew_Fiero||09-10-2001||11:33 PM||||how do i check to see if my rubber (no pun intended) has swelled? its oinly been a week and a bit, if i drain it all tomorrow, and fill the line iwth alcohol (with slave and master connected) and then fill and flush with dot 3<BR>do i have a chance? if not and i have to replace everytihng what are the steps?||||reg||1
Z||000012||theogre||09-10-2001||11:57 PM||||You have to replace/rebuild the MC and slave. You likely need to replace the rubber line at the slave. you have to flush that hard line.<P>the damage is already done. There is no other way to undo it. even a little oil damage is unacceptable and will eventually kill the system.<P>you probably haven't damaged anything else in the tranny yet.||||reg||1
Z||000013||artherd||09-12-2001||03:34 AM||||No, sorry.  a few hours is enough  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>All rubber will have to be replaced (rebuild both MC and slave, and replace flex line to the slave)<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Matthew_Fiero:<BR><B>how do i check to see if my rubber (no pun intended) has swelled? its oinly been a week and a bit, if i drain it all tomorrow, and fill the line iwth alcohol (with slave and master connected) and then fill and flush with dot 3<BR>do i have a chance? if not and i have to replace everytihng what are the steps?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
